This study employs molecular dynamics simulations to examine how solvent polarization influences the electric double layer (EDL) near a charged soft surface within an electrolyte solution. Our findings indicate that the EDL structure is highly sensitive to the dielectric constant of the solvent. Specifically, the polarization of both the grafted chains and the electrolyte molecules amplifies the conformational expansion of the grafted chains. Furthermore, the EDL composition is governed by the competition between the affinity of polarized electrolyte ions for the uncharged surface and the polarization-induced swelling of the uncharged region. These results offer valuable insights into the EDL structure at a charged soft surface within a polar solvent. Additionally, the observed phenomenon of polarization-induced chain expansion may have broader implications for understanding the behavior of soft matter systems. Finally, our observation that the EDL composition is dictated by the polarization of both the polymer and the ions underscores the critical role of solvent polarization in this system. These conclusions are supported by a theoretical analysis of the soft-sensing-soft-charge model. Overall, this research provides a comprehensive understanding of the EDL structure at a charged soft surface in an electrolyte solution containing a polar solvent. The study's findings have potential applications in various fields, including energy storage, drug delivery, and environmental remediation. Furthermore, the insights gained from this research can contribute to a deeper understanding of the fundamental principles governing the behavior of soft matter systems. The implications of this research extend beyond the specific system studied, as the principles discovered can be applied to a wide range of other soft matter systems. This study was supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China (Grant Nos. 11974277 and 11974278). The authors declare no conflicts of interest. The corresponding authors are Prof. Tao Li and Prof. Tao Sun.
